{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-48932",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(x)={r}^{′}(x)\\cdot s(x)+r(x)\\cdot {s}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(3)={r}^{′}(3)\\cdot s(3)+r(3)\\cdot {s}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs, we obtain that $r(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $s(3)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $r(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${r}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $s(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $1.$ So, ${s}^{′}(3)=1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(3) & ={r}^{′}(3)\\cdot s(3)+r(3)\\cdot {s}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
